github.com/digitorus/pdfsign v0.0.0-20260407063256-85ede6424a74 — modified by nib
==================================================================================

This directory is github.com/digitorus/pdfsign at v0.0.0-20260407063256-85ede6424a74, copied unchanged
from the Go module cache except for TWO files, verify/signature.go and verify/verify.go, and this notice.
go.mod replaces the module with this directory
(`replace github.com/digitorus/pdfsign => ./third_party/digitorus-pdfsign`); the require line still pins
that version. The upstream licence is LICENSE, unchanged (BSD 2-Clause, Digitorus).

Why: /pending 735, ADR-142.

What changed
------------

`processByteRange` (verify/signature.go) builds the bytes a signature covers, which the PKCS#7 library
then hashes whole from `p7.Content`. Upstream read every /ByteRange pair with `io.ReadAll` into a buffer
of its own — a buffer that grows as it fills, several times the bytes read by the time it is done — and
then appended that buffer to `p7.Content`, a second copy. One signature therefore allocated about six
times the bytes it covers: measured through nib's `sign.Verify`, 6.3, 6.1 and 7.1 times the file per
signature at 1, 4 and 16 MB.

The patched function reads the pairs straight into `p7.Content`, which is grown ONCE, to the number of
bytes the pairs can yield in a file of the size the caller already holds. Measured the same way after:
1.3, 1.1 and 1.0 times the file per signature.

`p7.Content` is still one whole slice holding every pair's bytes in order: github.com/digitorus/pkcs7
hashes it with a single `h.Write(p7.Content)` and offers no streaming entry point, so hashing the ranges
without holding them needs a patch to that module as well, and is not done.

The change in verify/verify.go is one argument: `VerifyWithOptions` passes the `size` it was given to
`processSignature`, which passes it on. Both functions gain that one parameter; both are unexported.

What is deliberately the same
-----------------------------

What is read, in what order, and every error are upstream's. Each pair still goes through
`io.NewSectionReader(file, offset, length)` and is read until that reader says it has ended, so every
quirk of a hostile /ByteRange is unchanged: a missing length reads as 0, a negative length reads to the
end of the file, a pair starting at or past the end reads nothing, a pair running past the end is
clamped, and a negative offset is the same `failed to read byte range N` error at the same pair. nib's
gate in front of this function (`internal/sign`, `libraryWouldOverread`) is written against exactly
those behaviours, and its tests pin them through this copy.

The file's size is used ONLY to choose the capacity. A capacity that is too small costs a further
allocation (the buffer then grows as `io.ReadAll` grows its own); it cannot change a byte that is read.

The declared divergences
------------------------

1. `v.Key("ByteRange")` is read once per signature, where upstream read it three times per pair. For a
direct array the value is the same. For an INDIRECT array upstream re-resolved and re-parsed the object
on every call; the patched function resolves it once. nib refuses an indirect /ByteRange before the
library is called, so this is reachable only by another caller of this copy.

2. On a read error part-way, `p7.Content` holds the bytes read so far including the failing pair's
partial read, where upstream held only the pairs before it. The caller drops `p7` on that error.

3. A full buffer asks the section for no bytes to learn whether it has ended, before growing. An
io.ReaderAt that answers such a read with neither bytes nor an end is then read as upstream read it.

Not changed: nothing under sign/, revocation/, cli/ or the module root, and no test. The library's own
tests in verify/ pass against the patched copy (`go test ./verify/` in this directory); nib's
`TestVerifyingASignatureAllocatesAboutWhatItCovers` (internal/sign) holds the allocation, and fails
against upstream's function.

Maintenance
-----------

Re-apply this patch at every bump of github.com/digitorus/pdfsign. The guard in nib's root package
(`TestTheSignatureLibraryPatchIsItsVerifyPathOnlyAsItsNoticeSays`) fails when go.mod's require moves off
this version, when the replace stops pointing here, when the patch is reverted, or when any file other
than verify/signature.go and verify/verify.go differs from the module-cache original.

Reporting upstream is Dan's call.
